description:
src/backend.rs:225 declares pub struct ParseResult without #[non_exhaustive]:

    pub struct ParseResult {
        pub parsed: HashMap<Id, Vec<CalendarEvent>>,
        pub not_found: Vec<Id>,
        pub not_parsable: Vec<Id>,
    }

The locked sister crate jmap-mail-server uses #[non_exhaustive] consistently (e.g. crate-jmap-mail-server/src/mdn.rs:20, :41). The locked jmap-types and jmap-base-client use it everywhere. ParseResult is exactly the kind of struct draft-ietf-jmap-calendars-26 may grow fields on (e.g. an iCalendar parse warnings vector, or a per-blob error map). Without #[non_exhaustive], any consumer who pattern-matches via  exhaustively breaks on field addition — a SemVer hazard for a pre-1.0 crate that is otherwise on the locked-API trajectory.

AvailabilityError at src/backend.rs:235 already has #[non_exhaustive] — good. ParseResult is the gap.

Note: CalendarEventGetArgs and CalendarEventQueryArgs (the ones added by JMAP-r3pg.2 and JMAP-r3pg.3) should also be marked #[non_exhaustive] from the start, since both will likely grow as drafts evolve.

Fix: add #[non_exhaustive] to ParseResult. If consumers need to construct it, add a constructor:
    impl ParseResult {
        pub fn new(parsed: ..., not_found: ..., not_parsable: ...) -> Self { ... }
    }
The locked mdn.rs precedent (line 33) is exactly this pattern.
